    {
      "ply": 54,
      "san": "Rff8",
      "human_san": "Rff8",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "This move makes matters worse \u2014 White now has a decisive advantage (+1.85 \u2192 +3.02, Stockfish:16), and White already has a queen and a pawn for a rook and a knight \u2014 extra material that typically decides middlegames. White punishes with e6 [pv:Rff8 e6 a6 Bf7 Bxe6]. Black missed the potential to keep fighting with Rfd7, which gives White a winning advantage [pv:Rfd7 Qa4 Be6 Qxa7 Ne7], a line that could leave a White piece hanging and let Black target the bishop on g6.",
      "system_symbol": "?",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": true,
      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 55,
      "san": "e6",
      "human_san": "e6",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "The most accurate move, e6, maintains White's advantage (+2.96, Stockfish:16), keeping Black's pieces passive, opening the e-file for the rook, and advancing the passed pawn dangerously\u2014a far-advanced passer often ties down the whole army. Deeper in the line White may target the knight on d5 and set the rooks to become more active on the open files [pv:e6 a6 Bf7 Bxe6 Bxe6]. Qc5 also gives White a winning advantage [pv:Qc5 Be6 Qxa7 Rd7 Rd1], as Black leaves a piece hanging and White targets the rook on f8, but e6 is clearly stronger for its lasting file and pawn pressure.",
      "system_symbol": "!",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": true,
      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 56,
      "san": "Ne7",
      "human_san": "Ne7",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "28...Ne7 gives White a winning advantage (+2.86, Stockfish:16). Black may have intended to redeploy the knight, but after [pv:Ne7 Bf7 Rc8 Qb3 Rc7] White's advantage only grows, and 29.Bf7 is the precise reply. The retreat fails to ease Black's defensive problems....a6 was a comparable option that also gives White a winning advantage [pv:a6 Bf7 Bxe6 Bxe6 Rfe8].",
      "system_symbol": null,
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": true,
      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 57,
      "san": "Bf7",
      "human_san": "Bf7",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "The most accurate move, 29.Bf7, maintains White's advantage (+3.07, Stockfish:16) and is set to leave Black with a hanging piece deeper in the line [pv:Bf7 Ra8 Qe4 Bf5 Qb4], since a hanging piece often forces costly rescue attempts. Qc4 is a roughly comparable option that likewise gives White a winning advantage [pv:Qc4 Nxg6 Qxg4 Ne7 Qb4], and if Black answers with...Rd4, White's grip on the game only tightens further.",
      "system_symbol": "!",
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": false,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": true,
      "neither": false
    },
    {
      "ply": 58,
      "san": "Rd4",
      "human_san": "Rd4",
      "san_match": true,
      "human_comment": "A mistake \nbut Vanek was in zeitnot... Time is a sharp weapon, which both the players\nhave. If one of them loses the possibility to use it ... that's bad for him!\nHowever Black's position was already compromised.",
      "human_symbol": null,
      "system_comment": "Black's 29...Rd4 makes matters worse, and White now has a decisive advantage (+3.07 \u2192 +6.14, Stockfish:16). The continuation [pv:Rd4 Qc5 Bxe6 Bxe6 Rdd8] only deepens Black's troubles, with Qc5 the most accurate way for White to press the advantage in the main line. Black missed the chance to play...Rc8, which still gives White a winning advantage [pv:Rc8 Qa4 Bf5 Qb4 Rc7] but keeps the evaluation materially more manageable.",
      "system_symbol": null,
      "is_key_moment": true,
      "final_comment": true,
      "both_present": true,
      "human_only": false,
      "system_only": false,
      "neither": false,
      "text_ratio": 0.0157,
      "text_jaccard": 0.0333
    },
